Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 10:54:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 057/154] feat: put a whole window or space slice back in one
 call
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every other sidebar verb is per-gesture: one row moves, one tab closes, one
cold tile reopens. Order, selection and folder membership have no verb at all,
so a restore assembled out of them would be long, racy and lossy. This takes
Rewind's slice WHOLE instead.

atrium_core_restore_slice validates the payload before it writes anything - a
space that does not exist, a scope the payload contradicts, a row with no URL,
and the document is byte-identical to what it was. A row whose live tab is
still around keeps its RowToken and is moved; a row that is gone is rewritten
cold, so reopening it adopts that row rather than adding a second.

The core hands back the half a document cannot do: which tabs to close, which
rows to reopen, and which tab to land on. RestoreSidebarSlice does them in that
order - closing first, so the strip never flashes the union of two states.

Selection arrives one of two ways, never both: a live tab id when the moment's
selected row survived, or a "selected" flag on one reopen entry when it did
not, because Chromium never reuses a tab id.

OpenSidebarColdTab takes `foreground` explicitly now: a restore reopens many
rows and only one of them may take the selection.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
